{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: forecasts are consistently above realized prices (typically +1 to +6 EUR, larger at longer horizons). Apply a downward level shift to the whole path, increasing with horizon.",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: in downtrends the model reacts too slowly and predicts stabilization/rebound too early. If last 10–15 days show a clear negative slope, enforce a continued decline for ~1–2 weeks before allowing flattening.",
    "Over-optimistic mean reversion: the model repeatedly pulls prices back toward mid/high-70s even when the market is breaking lower. Reduce mean-reversion strength when price is below recent 30d average and momentum is negative.",
    "Horizon-specific overshoot: errors grow with horizon; late-horizon forecasts often drift upward while outcomes keep falling. Use a horizon-dependent correction (e.g., small cut days 1–5, larger cut days 10–30).",
    "Volatility underestimation on selloffs: realized drawdowns are sharper than forecast paths. When recent downside volatility increases (large red days / widening range), steepen the forecast decline and widen uncertainty (or apply an extra downside adjustment)."
  ]
}
